'''''Glyceria notata''''', the '''plicate sweet-grass'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 }}</ref> or '''marked glyceria''', is a species of semi-aquatic true grass in the tribe [[Meliceae]]. It is from the cosmopolitan genus Glyceria ([[Glyceria|mannagrasses]]), a genus of tufted, perennial grasses. Its culms are 30–80&nbsp;cm in height, ascending from a prostrate base, with dark- to bluish-green, flat or folded leaf-blades some 5–30&nbsp;cm long by 3–14&nbsp;mm wide.

It is known as invasive in some parts of the world.

